<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/foundational/how-historians-work">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    History is not the past — it is <strong>the disciplined study of the past through its
    surviving evidence</strong>. The distinction matters: the past is gone; what remains is
    partial, biased, accidental in its survival. Historians build claims the way scientists build
    experiments — from sources, through method, with the reasoning exposed to challenge.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four-step method from artefact to argument" widget="equationStep" config={histCfg} />

  <h2>Skills that transfer everywhere</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reading against the grain:</strong> what does the tax record reveal about people it never mentions? Silences are evidence — usually about the powerful documenting only themselves.</li>
    <li><strong>Chronology and causation discipline:</strong> "earlier than" is not "because of". Post hoc reasoning is the field's original sin, and its ever-present temptation.</li>
    <li><strong>Presentism checked:</strong> judging the past by present values is sometimes necessary (slavery) and sometimes blinding (reading ancient motives through modern categories). The craft is knowing which you're doing.</li>
    <li><strong>Provisional conclusions:</strong> history rewrites itself when new sources surface (Soviet archives after 1991 rewrote the Cold War's early years). Any historian certain of everything has stopped doing history.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Carr, What Is History? (1961); Gaddis, The Landscape of History; Howell & Prevenier, From Reliable Sources.</p>
</TopicPage>
